# Hillier (1994) polarized electron-scattering benchmark.
#
# Validates STORM's polarized Thomson transport against the analytic
# single-scattering limit: null control, thin-limit amplitude, the sin^2 i
# inclination law, and Stokes u consistent with zero.  Self-contained -- no
# external reference data -- so it stands alone as a regression gate.
#
# Detectors are rings placed on the paper's inclinations (22.5, 45, 67.5, 90),
# 16 azimuths each plus their equatorial mirrors, accepting photons whose
# propagation direction is within 2 deg.  That cone is 1/25 the solid angle of a
# 10 deg one, so the generation count carries a matching factor: at 8 generations
# the 22.5 deg band would be ~50% noise.  128 generations puts the counted photon
# budget back where the wider cone had it.  STORM's SphericalObserver (--fibonacci)
# bins by crossing POSITION rather than direction and spreads its directions over
# the whole sphere, which forced a +-7 deg band; read that way i = 22.5 deg came
# out at half Hillier's value.  With rings it agrees, so all four inclinations are
# gated.
#
# One invocation runs both gating configurations -- the prolate envelope and the
# spherical null control -- so the harness must see two PASS lines.
#
# --sweep adds seven further chi0 points spanning two decades.  Those are NOT
# gating: most of that span is optically thick, where the analytic thin limit
# does not apply.  They exist so plot_hillier_fig5.py can lay the run over
# Hillier's published curves, and they cost a few seconds each.
#
# Requires -DSTORM_MONTECARLO_POLARIZATION=ON; without it the Stokes state is
# compiled out and the test cannot run.
TEST_ID="hillier_polarization"
TAGS="mpi"
CATEGORY="physics"
MPI_NP=16
RUN_ARGS=["--chi0", "0.02", "--generations", "128", "--tag", "0020", "--sweep"]
CHECK_FUNCTION="check_hillier_polarization_case"
TIMEOUT=3600
